<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/lib/Code.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
import Snippet from '#src/lib/Snippet.abide'
import layoutSource from '../../layout.abide' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="`log`">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Signature</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>log</code></td><td><code>(...args: unknown[]) =&gt; void</code></td><td>A message under the default namespace.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>log.info</code> / <code>log.warning</code> / <code>log.error</code> / <code>log.debug</code></td><td><code>(...args: unknown[]) =&gt; void</code></td><td>The four levels.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>log.named</code></td><td><code>(name: string) =&gt; Logger</code></td><td>A logger under a named namespace.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>Logger</code></td><td><code>typeof log</code></td><td>What <code>log.named</code> hands back.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>log.enabled</code></td><td><code>() =&gt; boolean</code></td><td>Whether a gated line under this namespace would be written.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>LogRecord</code></td><td><code>{'{ time: string; level: \'debug\' | \'info\' | \'warning\' | \'error\'; namespace: string; message: string; trace: string }'}</code></td><td>One line, as a value.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>log.records</code></td><td><code>Reactive&lt;LogRecord[]&gt;</code></td><td>The lines kept, appended to as each is written.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide:request</code></td><td><code>debug</code></td><td>One line per request.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide:socket</code></td><td><code>debug</code></td><td>One line per frame on the socket mux.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide:lifecycle</code></td><td><code>debug</code> <code>error</code></td><td>A lifecycle that ran.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide:principal</code></td><td><code>debug</code> <code>warning</code></td><td>A principal set or cleared.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide:health</code></td><td><code>warning</code></td><td>An <code>onHealth</code> that threw or answered a non-object.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide:mcp</code></td><td><code>debug</code> <code>warning</code></td><td>A handler withheld from MCP, or a tool published with no description.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide:openapi</code></td><td><code>warning</code></td><td>A handler whose schema had no JSON Schema export.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide:config</code></td><td><code>warning</code></td><td>A second <code>onConfig</code> replacing the first.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide:render</code></td><td><code>warning</code></td><td>An <code>error.abide</code> that itself failed.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide:refuse</code></td><td><code>warning</code></td><td>A message formatter that threw.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide:reactive</code></td><td><code>warning</code></td><td>A failed revalidation over a value still being served.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide:watch</code></td><td><code>warning</code></td><td>A <code>watch</code> effect or <code>Disposer</code> that threw.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide:hydrate</code> / <code>abide:navigate</code></td><td><code>warning</code></td><td>The browser lane.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Every line is appended to <code>log.records</code>, an ordinary <code>reactive</code>
        holding the lines kept as an array (28.1). It is trimmed back to
        <code>ABIDE_MAX_LOG_BUFFER_COUNT</code> lines, 1000 by default, once it holds twice that. It
        is read the way any value is read, so a page showing the latest lines subscribes rather than
        polls. In a template it is a member access, so the read is spelled out:
        <code>{'{#for line of log.records()}'}</code>.</p>

        <Section title="Namespaces and gating" level={3}>
            <p>A record carries a namespace, defaulting to <code>APP_NAME</code>. <code>DEBUG</code>
            gates which namespaces emit, with the same comma-and-wildcard grammar the ecosystem
            already uses — so a namespace nothing selects costs the argument evaluation and nothing
            else.</p>
        </Section>

        <Section title="The two formats" level={3}>
            <p>The readable form is for a terminal and the machine form is for a collector, chosen
            by <code>ABIDE_LOG_FORMAT</code>. <code>NO_COLOR</code> refuses ansi anywhere and
            <code>FORCE_COLOR</code> asks for the readable form off a pipe, which are the two
            conventions a terminal tool is expected to honor.</p>
        </Section>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`csp`">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Signature</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>csp</code></td><td><code>(sources?: Record&lt;string, string[]&gt;) =&gt; Middleware</code></td><td>The rung that sets <code>content-security-policy</code>.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>csp.nonce</code></td><td><code>() =&gt; string</code></td><td>This response's nonce, for the app's own inline script.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p><code>csp()</code> is a middleware rung, registered in the root layout like any other. It
        emits a policy that already knows what the build produced — the bundle's own chunks, the
        styles a component adopted — so the common case needs no directive written by hand.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`url`">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Signature</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>url</code></td><td><code>&lt;P extends RouteLiteral&gt;(url?: P, ...rest: HasParams&lt;P&gt; extends true ? [params: ParamsOf&lt;P&gt;, queryParams?: Query] : [params?: ParamsOf&lt;P&gt;, queryParams?: Query]) =&gt; string</code></td><td>Builds an address from a route literal.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>HasParams&lt;P&gt;</code></td><td><code>boolean</code></td><td>Whether the literal carries a required segment.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>HasSegments&lt;P&gt;</code></td><td><code>boolean</code></td><td>Whether the literal carries a segment of any kind.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>RequiredNames&lt;P&gt;</code></td><td><code>string</code></td><td>The required segment names.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>OptionalNames&lt;P&gt;</code></td><td><code>string</code></td><td>The optional segment names.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>RestNames&lt;P&gt;</code></td><td><code>string</code></td><td>The rest segment names.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>Query</code></td><td><code>Record&lt;string, unknown&gt; | URLSearchParams</code></td><td>The query half.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ParamsOf&lt;P&gt;</code></td><td><code>{'& { [K in RequiredNames<P>]: string | number } & { [K in OptionalNames<P>]?: string | number } & { [K in RestNames<P>]?: string | number | (string | number)[] }'}</code></td><td>What a route literal's segments accept.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>RouteParams&lt;P&gt;</code></td><td><code>{'{ [K in RequiredNames<P>]: string } & { [K in OptionalNames<P>]?: string } & { [K in RestNames<P>]: string[] }'}</code></td><td>What a page filed under <code>P</code> reads its params as.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>Routes</code></td><td><code>{'interface Routes {}'}</code></td><td>The app's route literals, which <code>abide check</code> declares.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>RouteLiteral</code></td><td><code>[keyof Routes] extends [never] ? string : Extract&lt;keyof Routes, string&gt;</code></td><td>What <code>url</code> takes.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <Section title="Parameters" level={3}>
            <ul>
                <li><code>pattern</code> — a <strong>route literal</strong>, not an address. The
                literal decides whether a params argument is required, optional or absent.</li>
                <li><code>params</code> — required where the literal carries a required segment. An
                omitted optional key omits its segment rather than leaving a hole.</li>
                <li><code>query</code> (optional) — a <code>Query</code>, and it is
                <strong>last</strong>, always.</li>
            </ul>
        </Section>

        <Section title="Return value" level={3}>
            <p>A mount-relative address, resolved at read time — against <code>&lt;meta
            name="abide-mount"&gt;</code> in a browser and against <code>config().APP_URL</code> on
            a server. That is what lets one build serve at the root and under a sub-path.</p>
        </Section>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`navigate`">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Signature</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>navigate</code></td><td><code>{'(url?: URL | string, options?: { replace?: boolean, keepScroll?: boolean }) => Promise<void>'}</code></td><td>Navigates.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>It navigates and does <strong>not reload the document</strong>, so layouts stay, loaded
        values stay loaded, and the head's keyed contributions are replaced rather than rebuilt. An
        ordinary <code>&lt;a href&gt;</code> does the same thing; <code>navigate</code> is for the
        case where the decision is made in code.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`reachable`">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Signature</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>reachable</code></td><td><code>(host: string) =&gt; Promise&lt;boolean&gt;</code></td><td>Whether a host answers at all.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>It is a server name, imported from <code>@abide/abide/server</code>, and it asks with one
        <code>HEAD</code>. Any answer counts — a 404 came from the host — so <code>false</code>
        means the request was refused, never resolved, or went 5 seconds unanswered. The answer is
        held for 60 seconds per host in one cache the whole process shares, which is the point:
        while a host is down, every call queued behind it refuses at once rather than each waiting
        out a timeout of its own.</p>

        <Section title="Parameters" level={3}>
            <ul>
                <li><code>host</code> — a bare host such as <code>api.example.com</code>, asked over
                https, or an origin such as <code>http://billing.internal:8080</code>, asked as
                written.</li>
            </ul>
        </Section>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Examples">
        <Section title="A log line with structure" level={3}>
            <Code language="ts" caption="server" code={`log.info('invoice paid', { id: '4310', cents: 124000 })`} />
        </Section>

        <Section title="A policy in the root layout" level={3}>
            <Snippet source={layoutSource} path="src/layout.abide" from={['middleware(csp(']} />
        </Section>

        <Section title="A refusal in place of a timeout" level={3}>
            <Code language="ts" caption="server" code={`if (!(await reachable('api.example.com')))
    return refuse(503, 'The catalog is unreachable')`} />
        </Section>

        <Section title="An address built from a literal" level={3}>
            <Code language="abide" caption="abide" code={`<a href={url('/invoices/[id]', { id: invoice.id })}>{invoice.number}</a>`} />
        </Section>
    </Section>

    <Section title="See also">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/app/record-what-happened')}>Logging</a> — the guide to
            <code>log</code></li>
            <li><a href={url('/app/lock-down-what-the-page-may-load')}>CSP</a> — the guide to
            <code>csp</code></li>
            <li><a href={url('/data/check-a-host-before-you-call-it')}>Reachable hosts</a> — the
            guide to <code>reachable</code></li>
            <li><a href={url('/pages/link-to-another-page')}>Links &amp; navigation</a> —
            <code>url</code> and <code>navigate</code> in use</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
